Hopefully this is a more informative response.  When faced with estimated equations that deal with different facets of a predicted outcome, I need to draw a picture.  Below is one way to look at the effects of each predictor.  (Note that I only have the "standardized" predictors and not the predictors in their original units.  If you supply the necessary values to "unstandardize" the predictors, I can make the display more informative.)  I've attached the Wolfram CDF (computable document format) code and a screenshot below:

Such displays can give you a "feeling" for the importance (or lack of importance) of each variable by moving the sliders.  (Categorical variables and confidence intervals can also be included.  Never go anywhere without some sort of measure of precision.)  The free CDF player can be downloaded at www.wolfram.com/cdf-player <http://www.wolfram.com/cdf-player> and works on Windows, Mac, and some flavors of Linux.  (I'm not associated with or promoting Wolfram products.  It just happens to be the software that I know how to use.  There's stuff in R-Studio that will accomplish the same thing.)  Oh...and it's a huge file to download: 2 or 3 gb but you only have to do it once.

Jim

